From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Manish KL <manish@example.com>
Date: Fri, 1 May 2026 00:00:00 +0530
Subject: [RFC PATCH 0/4] memory: hbf: introduce High Bandwidth Flash tier
 control plane

High Bandwidth Flash (HBF) is being standardized as a high-capacity,
high-bandwidth NAND-based tier for AI inference systems.  The expected
software problem is not merely exposing another block device: inference
runtimes need to stage token/KV/context data before compute demand, and
memory-management code needs enough semantic signal to avoid treating HBF
as a blind SSD spill tier.

This RFC introduces a small HBF control plane:

  1. a device class under /sys/class/hbf/
  2. a uapi ioctl for runtime-provided placement/prefetch hints
  3. an in-kernel registration API for future CXL/HBF or vendor drivers
  4. tracepoints for hint admission and completion

This first RFC deliberately avoids hard-coding vendor IDs, CXL DVSEC
layout, flash geometry, or DMA engines.  Those belong in the eventual
device-specific driver once an HBF specification is public.  The goal here
is to define the OS-facing contract: HBF is a warm, byte-addressable or
DMA-addressable AI memory tier, and runtimes should be able to tell the
kernel what objects are hot, cold, prefetchable, or demotable.

The intended future path is:

    AI runtime -> /dev/hbfctl hint -> HBF policy engine
        -> CXL/HBF driver -> DMA/prefetch/promotion/demotion
        -> HBM/DRAM/HBF placement

Patch layout:

  [1/4] uapi: add linux/hbf.h placement hint ABI
  [2/4] memory: hbf: add core class, registration API, and misc control dev
  [3/4] memory: hbf: add tracepoints for runtime hints
  [4/4] MAINTAINERS: add HBF entry

This is not a final hardware enablement patch. It is an LKML-style RFC
for discussion on whether HBF should be represented as a memory-tier
policy surface layered on top of CXL/DAX/mm migration rather than as a
new block-only device model.

---

